You are executing story S1 in epic E182 — the BOOTSTRAP story for the woof
deterministic-checks architecture.

S1 lands the producer/verifier/committer separation; S2-S4 depend on it.
S1 itself runs under the OLD pipeline (executor commits at end of /wf:execute-story);
the NEW pipeline (driver-owned commit, executor stages + writes
executor_result.json) takes effect from S2 onward. Do not apply the new
contract to your own execution — commit at the end as the existing skill
body instructs.

Read in order before coding:
  1. .woof/.current-epic                                  — verify E182
  2. .woof/epics/E182/plan.json                           — your story spec
  3. .woof/epics/E182/EPIC.md                             — 9 outcomes, 5 CDs, 12 ACs
  4. .woof/epics/E182/discovery/synthesis/PRINCIPLES.md   — P1-P6
  5. .woof/epics/E182/discovery/synthesis/ARCHITECTURE.md — registry, schemas, 9-check inventory, driver loop
  6. CLAUDE.md / AGENTS.md                                — project conventions (auto-loaded)

Then invoke /wf:execute-story with arguments "E182 S1".

CHECK-ID CANONICALISATION (orchestrator decision, not in any file):
  EPIC.md / plan.json / ARCHITECTURE.md use synthesis names:
  check_1_quality_gates, check_2_outcome_markers, check_3_scope,
  check_4_contract_refs, check_5_plan_crossrefs, check_6_critique_blocker,
  check_7_commit_transaction, check_8_docs_drift, check_9_review_valve.

  woof/schemas/gate.schema.json currently uses 6 different LEGACY names
  (check_1_quality_gate, check_2_spec_coverage, etc.). EPIC.md is
  post-Definition immutable; CD4 mandates the schema enum is registry-derived.
  SYNTHESIS NAMES WIN. Apply consistently across registry IDs, schema enum,
  runner filenames, test names, and prose. Add the executor-protocol triggers
  (subprocess_crash, executor_aborted, empty_diff_review) per CD4 notes.

NEW EXECUTOR CONTRACT (what you write, not what governs you):
  The new .claude/commands/wf/execute-story.md MUST NOT contain "git commit",
  "gate.md" as a write target, or any enumerated boundary-check list. O9
  asserts this via static-string test. Executor protocol per CD2: code, test,
  stage diff, write executor_result.json, exit 0. Driver-side does commit
  and gate.md.

NEW DRIVER LOOP (scripts/wf-run rewrite):
  After executor exit 0, read executor_result.json. Branch on outcome:
    staged_for_verification → run `woof check stage-5`; if check-result.ok,
                              git commit; else `woof gate write`.
    aborted_with_position    → `woof gate write --triggered-by executor_aborted`.
    empty_diff               → `woof gate write --triggered-by empty_diff_review`.
  Driver — never executor — invokes git commit. Driver writes gate.md only
  via `woof gate write` (never inline YAML).

REGRESSION FIXTURE (O7):
  tests/fixtures/woof/e181_s2/** uses .woof/epics/E181/critique/story-S2.md
  (severity: blocker) as input. Assertion: pipeline writes story_gate with
  check_6_critique_blocker in triggered_by[], no commit lands.

CHECK-6 SEMANTICS:
  Canonical (per ARCHITECTURE): "critique/story-S<k>.md exists; schema-valid;
  top-level severity equals max(findings[].severity); severity != blocker".
  NOT the OLD skill prose's "Dependencies satisfied" — that drift is what
  this epic eliminates.

REGISTRY COMPLETENESS:
  woof check stage-5 --self-test must enumerate all 9 registry slots and exit
  non-zero if any runner is unimplemented. In S1 only check_6_critique_blocker
  is real; checks 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 7, 8, 9 have placeholder runners that raise
  NotImplementedError. S2 fills 2/3/5/7; S3 fills 1/4/8/9.

Exit 0 only after the story commit transaction lands. Exit non-zero only after
writing .woof/epics/E182/gate.md.
